# biggs.dog — agent routing rules

Flux GitOps repo for a 2-cluster Kubernetes homelab (`clusters/cluster0` &
`clusters/cluster1`). The kagent MCP server (`https://mcp.biggs.dog/mcp`)
exposes specialized agents via `list_agents` (what's invocable now) and
`invoke_agent` (run a task on `ai-system/<name>`). Prefer the matching agent
over answering from memory or generic tools.

> ALWAYS call `list_agents` first if unsure what's live — the invocable set
> changes mid-migration. The table below documents intent, not a guarantee.

## MCP endpoint

`kagent-mcp` HTTPRoute on the internal LAN-only gateway `internal-biggs-dog`
(agentgateway, VIP `192.168.6.15`) → `Service/kagent-controller:8083`.
k8s-gateway split-horizon resolves `mcp.biggs.dog` to `.6.15` for LAN/VPN;
wildcard TLS, no auth; WAN clients 404. Read-only fallback:
`kubectl port-forward -n ai-system svc/kagent-controller 8083:8083`
(see `.scripts/test-agents.py`). Long runs (flux-agent ~70s) can exceed short
client timeouts — retry or use port-forward / direct `kubectl`.

## Model backend

All agents share one in-cluster vLLM backend (`vllm.ai-system:8000`,
OpenAI-compatible), served as `qwen 3.8 - local`
(`unsloth/Qwen3.8-27B-NVFP4`, dense 27B) on
`nv-01`'s RTX 5090. Inference never leaves the cluster. 173K context (262K
native, capped for KV headroom), fp8 KV cache, prefix caching, qwen3
tool/reasoning parsers. Cross-session vector memory is on (`vectorEnabled:
true`); flux-agent disables per-agent memory to stay under the window. Prefer
terse output.

## Two agent runtimes

1. **Declarative (python runtime)** — plain `Agent` CRs
   (`kubectl get agents -n ai-system`). Each runs its own Deployment, directly
   invocable. Stable path.
2. **Substrate SandboxAgents** — the ATE/actor runtime in
   `apps/ai-system/substrate/` (+ `-crds/`): `atelet` workers, `ate-controller`,
   `ate-api-server`, `atenet-router`. Five agents re-declared as `SandboxAgent`
   CRs in `apps/ai-system/kagent/substrate-agents/`, disabled in the chart so it
   can't also render them as python Agents. Support sandboxed code execution.

## Current state (verify before relying)

- **Invocable now (Declarative, stable):** `flux-agent`, `vm-agent`,
  `exa-agent`, `cilium-debug-agent`, `cilium-policy-agent`, `codebase-agent`.
- **Substrate SandboxAgents: not MCP-invocable.** `k8s-agent`,
  `helm-agent`, `promql-agent`, `observability-agent`, `cilium-manager-agent`.
  Two stacked blockers — use the fallbacks below / direct `kubectl`:

  1. **Version skew (resolved pending commit):** the substrate stack is at
     upstream **`v0.0.10`**, whose ate-controller calls `CreateAtespace`; the
     `ate-api-server` fork (`shrinedogg/ateapi`) was on older bases (first
     `v0.0.7` → `CreateAtespace` Unimplemented, then `v0.0.9` → `ateletpb` proto
     field-number skew vs the v0.0.10 ateom that surfaced as a cryptic
     `"string field contains invalid UTF-8"` unmarshal error and pinned every
     SandboxAgent at `Ready=ActorTemplateNotReady`). The fork is now rebased
     onto **v0.0.10** as `shrinedogg/ateapi:v0.0.10-jwksurl` (built+pushed;
     only the `--client-jwt-jwks-url` patch survives — v0.0.9 upstreamed the
     atelet-namespace fix as `installdefaults.NamespaceFromPodEnv()` reading
     `POD_NAMESPACE`). Pinned by digest in
     `apps/ai-system/substrate/app/helmrelease.yaml` (a mutable tag let the
     node cache serve stale builds). **Caveat:** the fork's base version MUST
     track the substrate chart version — a version skew silently breaks the
     golden-actor wire protocol. See
     `.wip/substrate-utf8-bug-report.md` for the full postmortem.
  2. **Upstream kagent MCP listing bug (blocks MCP invoke even when Ready):**
     `MCPHandler.listReadyAgents` lists only `v1alpha2.AgentList` (never
     `SandboxAgentList`) and hard-codes `condition.Reason == "DeploymentReady"`;
     substrate agents report Ready with reason `WorkloadReady`. Excluded twice
     (wrong CR type + wrong reason). The kagent REST API (`/agents`, kagent-ui)
     DOES list them once Ready is restored; only the MCP tool omits them. Fix
     needs a kagent change; bump kagent when it lands.

  Also: a stuck `Accepted=False` (`ActorTemplate ... spec: Invalid value:
  Spec is immutable`) can accumulate when a rendered ActorTemplate spec drifts;
  `kubectl delete actortemplate.ate.dev <name> -n ai-system` lets the
  reconciler recreate it (fixes Accepted, but do NOT do this while the version
  skew is unfixed — it triggers a golden rebuild that can't complete and flips
  Ready False).

  The substrate golden-actor stack was unblocked by a series of
  `ate-system`-vs-`ai-system` fixes (upstream hard-codes `ate-system`; this
  cluster runs `ai-system`) plus a Cilium egress gap. The notable final fix was
  `allow-egress-atelet-registries` (CNP, `app=atelet` → `world:443`) because the
  atelet pulls sandbox images in-process over the pod network (does NOT reuse
  node containerd). ai-system is Cilium default-deny; `world:443` egress is
  selectively allowed per labeled pod (kagent agents, vllm, embeddings, atelet,
  codebase-memory git-sync) — not cluster-wide.

To check live: `list_agents`, `kubectl get agents,sandboxagents -n ai-system`,
`kubectl logs -n ai-system deploy/ate-controller | grep -i reconciler`.

## Where agents are defined

- Chart-managed Agents — kagent `HelmRelease`
  (`apps/ai-system/kagent/app/helmrelease.yaml`), toggled via per-agent
  `*.enabled` values. Flux owns these; disable there, not `kubectl delete`.
  (`argo-rollouts-agent`, `istio-agent`, `kgateway-agent` are disabled.)
- `flux-agent` — `apps/ai-system/flux-mcp/` (read-only Flux).
- `vm-agent` — `apps/ai-system/victoria-metrics-mcp/`.
- `exa-agent` — `apps/ai-system/exa-mcp/`.
- `cilium-debug-agent`, `cilium-policy-agent` —
  `apps/ai-system/kagent/cilium-agents/` (chart versions disabled; re-defined
  statically to carry generic `k8s_*` tools alongside Cilium tools).
- `codebase-agent` + `codebase-memory-mcp` server —
  `apps/ai-system/codebase-memory-mcp/` (see below).
- `k8s-agent`, `helm-agent`, `promql-agent`, `observability-agent`,
  `cilium-manager-agent` — `apps/ai-system/kagent/substrate-agents/`
  (SandboxAgents; degraded — see above).

Keep this list in sync with the live set.

### codebase-memory-mcp (new)

Code-intelligence MCP server (tree-sitter → SQLite knowledge graph + semantic
search) indexing the **biggs.dog** repo, plus `codebase-agent`. The upstream
image is **stdio-only** (no HTTP transport), so the pod wraps it in an
`mcp-proxy` stdio→streamable-HTTP bridge and runs **git-sync as a native
sidecar** (`restartPolicy: Always` initContainer) to keep a `main` checkout at
`/repo/current`. Notes: glibc binary + musl bridge image → binary AND glibc
runtime are staged via an initContainer and spawned through the copied loader;
mcp-proxy is used (not supergateway) because supergateway rejects the
spec-mandated `notifications/initialized` kagent sends (406) → RemoteMCPServer
ReconcileFailed; git-sync must be a native sidecar (regular containers start
only after ALL initContainers finish → deadlock with the `wait-for-repo`
init); git creds come from ExternalSecret `codebase-memory-git` (1Password
item, fields `username` + `password` = read-only GitHub PAT); egress via CNP
`allow-egress-codebase-memory-github`.

## Agent selection

Pick the single most specific agent. ✅ = invocable now, ⚠️ = degraded (use
the fallback).

- ✅ **flux-agent** — Flux CD / GitOps inspection via the Flux Operator MCP:
  `FluxInstance`, sources, `Kustomization`s, `HelmRelease`s, `ResourceSet`s, +
  reconciliation root-cause and Flux docs. Read-only. Use for "why isn't this
  reconciling / syncing from git". Best for live `HelmRelease` state while
  `helm-agent` is down.
- ✅ **vm-agent** — Direct VictoriaMetrics: PromQL/MetricsQL, metrics/labels/
  series discovery, alerting/recording rules, TSDB cardinality, unused/high-
  cardinality metrics, slow queries, embedded VM docs. Also the fallback for
  metric queries + PromQL authoring while `observability-agent`/`promql-agent`
  are down.
- ✅ **exa-agent** — Web search, code discovery, company/competitor research,
  technical-docs discovery, web intelligence. Requires an Exa API key.
- ✅ **cilium-debug-agent** — Diagnosing a broken/degraded Cilium install:
  connectivity failures, dropped traffic, Hubble flow inspection. Carries
  generic `k8s_*` tools → doubles as general cluster-inspection agent while
  `k8s-agent` is down.
- ✅ **cilium-policy-agent** — Authoring `CiliumNetworkPolicy` /
  `CiliumClusterwideNetworkPolicy` from natural-language intent. Only when the
  deliverable is a policy manifest. Also carries `k8s_*` tools.
- ✅ **codebase-agent** — Structural code intelligence over indexed repos
  (currently biggs.dog, auto-synced from `main`): find symbols, trace
  callers/callees & data flow, architecture overviews, blast-radius of a diff,
  read exact source. Use for "where is X defined / what calls this / what
  breaks if I change this".
- ⚠️ **k8s-agent** — General K8s ops/inspection/troubleshooting. Default for
  "what's wrong in the cluster". **Fallback:** `cilium-debug-agent` or direct
  `kubectl`.
- ⚠️ **observability-agent** — Monitoring: metrics, Grafana dashboards
  (grafana-mcp), alerting, cluster↔metric correlation. **Fallback:** `vm-agent`
  for metrics; Grafana work waits for the actor.
- ⚠️ **promql-agent** — Generate a PromQL query from natural language.
  **Fallback:** `vm-agent`.
- ⚠️ **helm-agent** — Helm release management/troubleshooting. This repo drives
  Helm via Flux `HelmRelease`, so edit the `HelmRelease` YAML regardless.
  **Fallback:** `flux-agent` for live state + direct `helm`/`kubectl`.
- ⚠️ **cilium-manager-agent** — Installing/configuring/upgrading/monitoring
  Cilium ("set up / change Cilium"). **Fallback:** `cilium-debug-agent` for
  inspection; make config changes by editing manifests.

## Repo conventions

- Desired state lives in Git (`clusters/cluster*/**`); Flux reconciles it. Use
  agents to inspect/diagnose *live* state, but make persistent changes by
  editing YAML here, not by mutating the cluster.
- Secrets via External Secrets + 1Password; never hardcode credentials. (Where
  a 1Password item doesn't exist yet, a temporary in-cluster Secret + a
  post-renderer env patch is used — e.g. `kagent-grafana-mcp-token`; replace
  with an ExternalSecret once the item exists.) ExternalSecret `remoteRef`
  field names must match the 1Password item's field labels exactly.
- Flux tracks `main`. Pushing to a feature branch won't reconcile — merge to
  `main`.
